 "introductionText": "What FRUS answers directly: the printed record shows corruption shaping U.S. decisions in four recurring ways: as a reason to limit or condition aid to a client government (China 1947–48, the Philippines 1950), as an obstacle the United States declined to remove by taking control (South Vietnam 1960–68), as a diplomatic problem when American firms' bribes threatened allied governments (Lockheed and Northrop, 1975–76), and as a domestic scandal that constrained policy (Koreagate and the 1978 troop withdrawal). Where FRUS points for the rest: the documents came mainly from the Department of State's central files (Record Group 59), from lot files such as the Records of Henry Kissinger (Entry A1 5403) and Richard N. Cooper's subject files (Entry UD-04D 21/22), and from the Ford and Carter Libraries' national security files; the editors also cite unprinted Executive Secretariat and Policy Planning lots. What this request cannot get from FRUS: the congressional investigations' own records, the drafting of the Foreign Corrupt Practices Act, and anything after 1988.",
