   "text": "2. Corruption as an obstacle in South Vietnam, and the limits of leverage (1960–1968)",
   "level": 1
  },
  {
   "kind": "prose",
   "text": "From Durbrow in 1960 to the Saigon embassy's corruption committee in 1967, U.S. officials treated corruption in the South Vietnamese government as a cause of political weakness and an obstacle to pacification; Calhoun's 1967 memorandum is the clearest statement of why Washington declined the control that a rapid cure would have required."
  },
  {
   "kind": "document",
   "volumeId": "frus1958-60v01",
   "documentId": "d179",
   "notes": [
    "Agent finding, run s9, 2026-10-30: Durbrow's 1960 letter lists Diem's tolerance of corruption in his family and in high circles among the weaknesses of his rule."
   ]
  },
  {
   "kind": "excerpt",
   "volumeId": "frus1958-60v01",
   "documentId": "d179",
   "text": "Diem knowingly tolerated corruption in his family and in high governmental circles though remaining personally free of such taint."
  },
  {
   "kind": "document",
   "volumeId": "frus1964-68v05",
   "documentId": "d258",
   "notes": [
    "Agent finding, run s9, 2026-10-30: Calhoun accepts that corruption has become a key obstacle to pacification but rejects a U.S. veto over South Vietnamese appointments as an invasion of sovereignty; the editors' note records the committee's 'incident reports' compromise."
   ]
  },
  {
   "kind": "excerpt",
   "volumeId": "frus1964-68v05",
   "documentId": "d258",
   "text": "I agree that the level of corruption in Viet-Nam has reached a point where, as you point out, it has become a key obstacle to pacification."
  },
  {
   "kind": "excerpt",
   "volumeId": "frus1964-68v05",
   "documentId": "d258",
   "text": "The assumption of such sweeping prerogatives entails an invasion of the sovereignty of the Republic of Viet-Nam so great that it could and would be argued thereafter that United States is indeed the neo-colonialist power its critics and enemies allege it to be."
  },
  {
   "kind": "document",
   "volumeId": "frus1964-68v06",
   "documentId": "d16",
   "notes": [
    "Agent finding, run s9, 2026-10-30: DePuy's January 1968 memorandum records that corrupt province chiefs had not been removed and junior officers were restive over corruption in the armed forces."
   ]
  },
  {
   "kind": "excerpt",
   "volumeId": "frus1964-68v06",
   "documentId": "d16",
   "text": "The Junior Officers of the Army are increasingly restive in that nothing has been done about corruption in the Armed Forces."
  },
  {
   "kind": "heading",
   "text": "3. American corporate bribery abroad as a diplomatic problem (1975–1976)",
   "level": 1
  },
  {
   "kind": "prose",
   "text": "When the Senate's multinational corporations subcommittee exposed Lockheed's and Northrop's payments, the Department and the White House handled the disclosures as a threat to allied governments: in West Germany and the Netherlands, in Saudi Arabia, and in Japan, where the Prime Minister asked for the names and the embassy watched a former Prime Minister's arrest."
  },
  {
   "kind": "document",
   "volumeId": "frus1969-76ve15p2",
   "documentId": "d292",
   "notes": [
